  "summary": "The 88×31 pixel button wall: identity at 2,728 pixels.",
  "description": "The 88x31 pixel button is old web's unit of identity: tiny banners — \"Made with Notepad\", \"Netscape Now!\", \"Best viewed at 800x600\", site-specific pixel art — collected into button walls and stitched together by webrings, circular link exchanges that let strangers' homepages reference each other like quotes in a guestbook. The format's constraints (two colors, 6-8px type, 1px bevel) produced a craft dialect of micro-design; the webring produced an etiquette of mutual visibility. The 2020s neocities/yesterweb revival rebuilt both: button-making tutorials, ring syndicates, and collectors documenting thousands of vintage badges as design history.",
  "culturalContext": "The 88x31 button and webring were the old web's social fabric: link-exchange etiquette built strangers' sites into networks, the 2020s neocities revival rebuilt both as craft practice, and badge archives document them as the pre-algorithm web's design history.",
